'''Nolan North''' {{born|31|October|1970}} is an actor and voice performer who had a supporting role as a [[Vengeance bridge officer 001|''Vengeance'' bridge officer]] in {{film|12}} and performed several voices in {{s|LD}}. According to {{TrekMovie.com}} North was asked by Abrams if he would work on the ''Trek'' sequel while working on [[J.J. Abrams]]' {{wt|Super 8 (2011 film)|Super 8}} (2011), and North mentioned that it was a very small role. {{TrekMovie.com|2012/05/21/jj-abrams-put-uncharteds-north-into-star-trek-sequel-tried-to-get-chess-master-carlsen/}}

Born in New Haven, Connecticut, USA, North is known for his work in various projects, including on the game series {{wt|Uncharted}} (beginning in 2007) where he voiced the main character {{w|Nathan Drake}}, (with [[Richard McGonagle]] as the voice of Drake's mentor Victor Sullivan). North also made an appearance in the 2022 film, {{wt|Uncharted (film)|Uncharted}}, and provided voice work for the film's novelization audiobook. North also portrayed protagonist {{w|List of Assassin's Creed characters#Desmond Miles|Desmond Miles}} in multiple entries of the near-annual {{wt|Assassin's Creed}} franchise (also beginning in 2007, with that character's father, {{w|List of Assassin's Creed characters#William Miles|William Miles}}, being voiced by [[John de Lancie]]).

North's other voice-over work includes the recurring role of {{w|Penguin (character)|Oswald Cobblepot}}, aka The Penguin, in the video games {{wt|Batman: Arkham City}} (with [[James Horan]] and [[Tasia Valenza]]), {{wt|Batman: Arkham Origins}} (with [[Mark Rolston]]), {{wt|Batman: Arkham Knight}} (2015, with [[Jonathan Banks]], [[Robin Atkin Downes]], James Horan, [[Loren Lester]], Mark Rolston, [[Dwight Schultz]], [[Keith Silverstein]], [[Keith Szarabajka]], Tasia Valenza, and [[Marc Worden]]), {{wt|Suicide Squad: Kill the Justice League}} (2024, with [[Jason Isaacs]]), and {{wt|Batman: Arkham Shadow}} (2024). North also voiced the same character in the animated feature {{wt|Batman: Assault on Arkham}} (2014, with [[Neal McDonough]]).

